WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.240
What you actually get when a model is “open”.

2
00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:16.685
When a lab releases an open model, you typically download three things: a set of weights (the learned parameters, often tens or hundreds of gigabytes), a config describing the architecture, and a tokenizer with its chat template.

3
00:00:16.965 --> 00:00:22.747
With an inference library you can run it on your own hardware, inspect it, quantize it, and fine-tune it.

4
00:00:23.027 --> 00:00:28.083
That is a lot of freedom compared with an API, where you only see text in and text out.

5
00:00:28.363 --> 00:00:35.019
What you usually don’t get is how the model was made: the training data, the data filtering code, and the training code.

6
00:00:35.299 --> 00:00:39.459
That gap is why careful people say open-weight rather than open source.

7
00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:43.856
Think of it like receiving a compiled binary under a generous license.

8
00:00:44.136 --> 00:00:50.557
You can run it, patch it, and redistribute it, but you can’t rebuild it from source, and you can’t audit what went into it.

9
00:00:50.837 --> 00:00:59.563
The Open Source Initiative, the body that maintains the Open Source Definition for software, published the Open Source AI Definition 1.

10
00:00:59.843 --> 00:01:02.403
0 in October 2024.

11
00:01:02.683 --> 00:01:16.357
It carries the four software freedoms over to AI systems: freedom to use the system for any purpose without asking permission, to study how it works, to modify it for any purpose, and to share it, modified or not.

12
00:01:16.637 --> 00:01:33.405
To make those freedoms real, it asks for the “preferred form for making modifications,” which has three parts: data information detailed enough that a skilled person could build a substantially equivalent system (provenance, scope, collection, filtering, and a list of sources),

13
00:01:33.685 --> 00:01:37.184
the complete code for training and inference, and the parameters.

14
00:01:37.464 --> 00:01:41.411
By that standard, most popular open models are open-weight only.

15
00:01:41.691 --> 00:01:43.419
A few publishers go further.

16
00:01:43.699 --> 00:01:47.411
Ai2 releases its Olmo models under Apache 2.

17
00:01:47.691 --> 00:01:57.675
0 along with the Dolma pretraining data and its training code, which is why researchers use Olmo when they need to study how training choices affect behavior.
